    {
      "blob": "a80c6b168bf98c75c45c7ebb016d3cf7ed720ae2",
      "paths": [
        "bhoys eyes part 1 v1.1.2.docx",
        "data/research/grants/applications/gary_aureon_metadata_research_evidence_20260712_20260712_203741/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2_8c7d1e5541.docx",
        "docs/bhoys eyes part 1 v1.1.2.docx",
        "docs/research/misc/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2.docx",
        "imports/Kimi_Agent_Aureon_20260408/aureon-trading-main-snapshot/docs/research/misc/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2.docx"
      ],
      "line": 1515,
      "text": "Conversations became more guarded. Mr. Thompson, chatting with a neighbour outside his hardware store, kept his voice low and his sentences short. \"You never know who’s listening,\" he said, his eyes darting to the camera perched above the streetlight. The neighbour nodded, understanding the unspoken need for caution. Their discussions, once filled with animated debate and laughter, now felt stilted and uneasy."
    },
    {
      "blob": "a80c6b168bf98c75c45c7ebb016d3cf7ed720ae2",
      "paths": [
        "bhoys eyes part 1 v1.1.2.docx",
        "data/research/grants/applications/gary_aureon_metadata_research_evidence_20260712_20260712_203741/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2_8c7d1e5541.docx",
        "docs/bhoys eyes part 1 v1.1.2.docx",
        "docs/research/misc/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2.docx",
        "imports/Kimi_Agent_Aureon_20260408/aureon-trading-main-snapshot/docs/research/misc/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2.docx"
      ],
      "line": 1575,
      "text": "The weight of his decisions pressed down on him. Each mission, each piece of intelligence gathered, had felt like a step towards a noble cause. But now, in the solitude of his room, the noble cause seemed intertwined with threads of fear and regret. He remembered the look on his mother's face the last time they had spoken, her eyes filled with a mix of pride and worry. \"Be careful, Michael,\" she had said, her voice trembling slightly. \"Promise me you’ll stay safe.\" He had nodded, unable to fully meet her gaze."
    },
    {
      "blob": "a80c6b168bf98c75c45c7ebb016d3cf7ed720ae2",
      "paths": [
        "bhoys eyes part 1 v1.1.2.docx",
        "data/research/grants/applications/gary_aureon_metadata_research_evidence_20260712_20260712_203741/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2_8c7d1e5541.docx",
        "docs/bhoys eyes part 1 v1.1.2.docx",
        "docs/research/misc/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2.docx",
        "imports/Kimi_Agent_Aureon_20260408/aureon-trading-main-snapshot/docs/research/misc/bhoys_eyes_part_1_v1.1.2.docx"
      ],
      "line": 2174,
      "text": "The soldiers' footsteps grew louder, the gap between them closing rapidly. Michael could feel the tension in the air, the unspoken understanding that any misstep could lead to disaster. His grip tightened slightly on the strap of his bag, the concealed weapons a heavy burden both physically and mentally."
    },
    {
      "blob": "559088232b07d58eb805826512dea7ea1c00221f",
      "paths": [
        "Antarctic research/The_Full_Story_v1 (1).docx",
        "data/research/grants/applications/gary_aureon_metadata_research_evidence_20260712_20260712_203741/The_Full_Story_v1_1_a671950d3b.docx"
      ],
      "line": 49,
      "text": "Seven hundred years after Carnac, Ireland's Boyne Valley becomes the densest substrate node yet built. Newgrange, Knowth, and Dowth are constructed within roughly the same century. Newgrange is the most architecturally sophisticated. Its passage runs nineteen metres into the cairn. A small slot above the entrance — the roof-box — was deliberately cut to admit the rising sun on the morning of the winter solstice into the central chamber. The roof-box is calibrated. If it had been cut twenty centimetres too high or too low, or if the passage had been built a few metres shorter or longer, the alignment would have failed. It does not fail."
    },
    {
      "blob": "559088232b07d58eb805826512dea7ea1c00221f",
      "paths": [
        "Antarctic research/The_Full_Story_v1 (1).docx",
        "data/research/grants/applications/gary_aureon_metadata_research_evidence_20260712_20260712_203741/The_Full_Story_v1_1_a671950d3b.docx"
      ],
      "line": 97,
      "text": "By 2500 BCE the Atlantic substrate has built a chain of solstice-and-equinox-aligned monuments from southern Iberia at 38°N to the Northern Isles at 59°N. The kindred network is documented genetically across the chain. The architecture is calendrically calibrated to the working of solar and lunar bodies in the sky at the substrate epoch. The home base of operations is the threshold itself — the threshold being not just the architectural sites but the maritime kindred network that linked them, the calendar mathematics that timed them, and the carrier-class that operated them."
    },
    {
      "blob": "6a76d2415c2c3cf0081a136291db4fce4ada0cc7",
      "paths": [
        "Antarctic research/From_A_to_B_v1-1.docx",
        "Antarctic research/From_A_to_B_v1.docx",
        "data/research/grants/applications/gary_aureon_metadata_research_evidence_20260712_20260712_203741/From_A_to_B_v1-1_a857947484.docx"
      ],
      "line": 57,
      "text": "Seven hundred years after Carnac, Ireland's Boyne Valley becomes the densest substrate node yet built. Newgrange, Knowth, and Dowth — three great passage tombs within five kilometres of each other — are constructed within roughly the same century. Newgrange is the most architecturally sophisticated. Its passage runs nineteen metres into the cairn. A small slot above the entrance — the roof-box — was deliberately cut to admit the rising sun on the morning of the winter solstice into the central chamber. The roof-box is calibrated. If it had been cut twenty centimetres too high or too low, or if the passage had been built a few metres shorter or longer, the alignment would have failed. It does not fail. For five mornings around the winter solstice each year, the sun enters the chamber for approximately seventeen minutes."
    },
